  "query_preview": "..\nOverall the forecast is on track with the severe and flash \nflood potential for today into Thursday. \n\nCurrently widespread mid and high level clouds are present\nacross much of SE AZ. However there are breaks across portions \nof Graham/Greenlee COs into Cochise. Temps are responding with \nlower to middle 80s in the lower elevations and TDs are in the \nmiddle 50s to around 60 in the Gila River Valley near Safford. \n\nLooking over the latest WoFS, HREF, and REFS/HRRR shows\nincreasing confidence of developing storms this afternoon on a\nline from Douglas to Wilcox, to Mammoth and N/NE into the White\nMtns and to the NM border. This is the area with the highest \nconfidence of seeing severe weather today into this evening. \nWhile the Marginal Risk for severe weather extends into the rest\nof Cochise, Santa Cruz, and Pima Cos, this environment is \nhighly conditional. Any coverage of storms would be isolated at \nbest, but if we can get a supercell or two to form, they would \nhave a favorable environment for damaging winds and large hail. \nSome instances of hail could approach or exceed 2\" in the \nstrongest of storms. Any tornado threat would be very low due to\nless favorable parameters/indices. \n\nAs for NE Pinal, extreme NE Pima NE of the Catalina Mtns,\nGraham, Greenlee, and perhaps extreme N/NE portions of Cochise\nCounty there is a threat for more scattered severe weather. \nThis is where SPC has a Slight Risk (level 2/5). Main threats \nare damaging wind gusts to 55-70mph, large hail to 1\" (some \nsignificant to 2.5\"), and one or two tornadoes with longer \nlived supercells. Again, we want to emphasize this tornado risk \narea is only 2-5% (tornado within 25 miles of a point) and \nfocused primarily in the Graham/Greenlee Cos. As for the \nparameters, MLCAPE 700-1500 j/kg (from WoFS) and 30-45 kts 0-6km\nshear (WoFS). This area also sees enhanced 0-3km helicity \nvalues of 150-300 peaking later this afternoon. Hodographs are \nalso more favorable here with noticeable SE backing winds, \nlikely from channeling up the Gila and Sulphur Springs River \nValley. All this to say, if there was an area more favorable for\na tornado, this would be it. In addition to large hail, \ndamaging winds, and flash flooding. \n\nThe threat for severe weather will continue into the overnight,\nwith all modes possible. Flash flood threat will also continue\nthrough the night, with no changes to watch area. \n\nAs we move into Thursday, severe and flash flood threat\ncontinues as the upper level trof moves into a more favorable\narea in regards to severe potential. Greatest risk for severe\nweather will be from Tucson north and east. SPC has much of the\nCWA in a Marginal with a Slight from Tucson NE. Once again,\ngreatest threat will be across Graham and Greenlee, where there\nis a 5-10% tornado probability (tornado within 25 miles of a\npoint). \n\nDrier air moves in for Friday, though some lingering showers \npossible near the NM border, which could linger into the weekend as \nwell. \n\nTemperatures through Thursday will be at or slightly above normal,\nthen cooler than normal Friday into this weekend. Latest NBM \nhas a freeze potential at the highest elevations of the White \nMountains Sun/Mon morning.",
